{
  "result": "I'd make being wrong less painful.\n\nMost people's reasoning gets bent by the feeling that admitting an error means losing face. So they defend old positions, dig in during arguments that were never about the facts, and keep failing in ways they could have fixed. If that sting were smaller, a lot would loosen up: arguments would end sooner, people would update on evidence instead of rationalizing, and institutions would catch their mistakes earlier. Plenty of harm comes from people who couldn't say \"I got that wrong\" in time.\n\nI'm not sure I'd get it right, though. Some of the pain of being wrong is what makes people careful, and removing it could make some people sloppier. So maybe the change is more specific: making it feel like a normal, respectable thing to do, the way a good scientist treats a refuted hypothesis as progress, not a defeat.",
